{"causes":["The rise of the Italian Renaissance provided a cultural and intellectual context in which Ludovico Ariosto could create his masterpiece.","The influence of medieval romances and classical mythology on Ariosto's work helped shape the epic poem's narrative and themes.","The patronage of the Este family, who commissioned the poem's creation, played a significant role in its production and dissemination."],"effects":["Orlando Furioso helped establish the vernacular language as a viable alternative to Latin in Italian literature.","The poem's influence can be seen in the development of the English epic poem, with works such as John Milton's Paradise Lost drawing on Ariosto's innovations.","The poem's themes of love, honor, and loyalty have continued to resonate with readers and inspire artists across centuries."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"ludovico-ariosto","sourceName":"Ludovico Ariosto","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"orlando-furioso","targetName":"Orlando Furioso","context":"Ariosto's magnum opus, Orlando Furioso, is a seminal work of Italian literature."},{"sourceSlug":"italian-renaissance","sourceName":"Italian Renaissance","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"italy","targetName":"Italy","context":"Orlando Furioso reflects the cultural and intellectual currents of the Italian Renaissance."},{"sourceSlug":"medieval-romances","sourceName":"Medieval Romances","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"orlando-furioso","targetName":"Orlando Furioso","context":"Ariosto drew inspiration from medieval romances in crafting the narrative of Orlando Furioso."},{"sourceSlug":"este-family","sourceName":"Este Family","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"orlando-furioso","targetName":"Orlando Furioso","context":"The Este family commissioned the creation of Orlando Furioso, helping to bring the poem to a wider audience."},{"sourceSlug":"english-epic-poem","sourceName":"English Epic Poem","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"paradise-lost","targetName":"Paradise Lost","context":"Orlando Furioso's influence can be seen in the development of the English epic poem, with works such as John Milton's Paradise Lost drawing on Ariosto's innovations."},{"sourceSlug":"artistic-inspiration","sourceName":"Artistic Inspiration","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"orlando-furioso","targetName":"Orlando Furioso","context":"The poem's themes and characters continue to inspire artists, writers, and musicians to this day."},{"sourceSlug":"literary-innovation","sourceName":"Literary Innovation","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"italian-literature","targetName":"Italian Literature","context":"Orlando Furioso helped establish the vernacular language as a viable alternative to Latin in Italian literature."}],"places":[{"name":"Rome","role":"Commissioning city"},{"name":"Vergi","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Bologna","role":"Publication city"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Orlando Furioso","type":"Reference article","slug":"orlando-furioso"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q48922","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orlando_Furioso"],"tags":["literary work","Epic Poetry & Mythology","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Orlando Furioso contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Orlando Furioso ('Raging Roland') is an Italian Renaissance epic poem by Ludovico Ariosto (1474–1533), first published in 40 cantos in 1516 and completed in its definitive 46-canto form in 1532 — one of the longest poems in European literature at approximately 38,736 lines of ottava rima. The poem continues Matteo Maria Boiardo's unfinished Orlando Innamorato (c. 1495) and narrates the adventures of the paladins of Charlemagne in their wars against the Saracens, centred on the knight Orlando (Roland of the Chanson de Roland) who falls into a mad rage (the furioso) after the Saracen princess Angelica, whom he loves, elopes with the common soldier Medoro. The poem's multiple interwoven plotlines involve the knight Ruggiero (ancestor of the Este dynasty of Ferrara) and the female warrior Bradamante, the sorcerer Atlante's enchanted palace, the hippogryph, Astolfo's voyage to the Moon to recover Orlando's lost wits in a vial, and dozens of other episodes of love, war, magic, and adventure.

Orlando Furioso is the supreme achievement of the Italian Renaissance romanzo — a form that combines the classical epic tradition (the war against external enemies) with the medieval romance tradition (individual adventure, love, magic, quest) in a consciously playful, ironic, humanist register. Ariosto's poem is distinguished by its narrative sophistication — the interlaced structure (entrelacement) that interrupts stories at moments of maximum tension to pursue other threads — and its constantly self-aware, witty narrator who comments on the action, addresses the reader directly, and makes clear that the poem is a literary artifice as much as a heroic narrative.

Orlando Furioso is one of the most influential texts in European literary history — it influenced Edmund Spenser's Faerie Queene (1590–1596), William Shakespeare (Much Ado About Nothing, Othello), Miguel de Cervantes (Don Quixote engages directly with the romanzo tradition Ariosto perfected), and George Frideric Handel (Orlando, 1733; Alcina, 1735). The poem's complex, ironic narration and its self-aware critique of heroic convention anticipate the modern novel in important ways.","subjects":["Italian Literature","Renaissance Era","Ludovico Ariosto","Epic Poetry","Romance Literature","Italian Renaissance","European Literature","Opera"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Orlando Furioso is a foundational work of Italian literature that reflects the cultural and intellectual currents of the Italian Renaissance.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
